rate, even some metabolic rate changes. Studies have shown that certain meditative practices can improve overall asthma *management*, reduce the frequency of mild exacerbations, and help with anxiety-induced hyperventilation. But there is **no evidence** that a meditator can abort a genuine, moderate‑severe asthma attack without medication. If such a thing were possible, it would have been published as a landmark finding, and every pulmonologist would be studying it. The reality is, when researchers test the limits of biofeedback on airway smooth muscle, the effects are tiny and only relevant in stable, non‑attacking conditions. During an established attack, the local inflammatory cascade has rendered the nervous system’s fine-tuning irrelevant. --- ### 5. The ultimate “why” Because the airway muscle in an asthma attack isn’t just “tense”; it’s **chemically locked in spasm** by compounds your conscious mind cannot generate or neutralize. You can’t will your mast cells to stop releasing histamine any more than you can will your white blood cells to stop attacking an infection. It’s a cellular‑level immune event, not a nervous‑system shift. So back to your cousin: She’s likely a master of intercepting the very earliest stage, where the chemical storm is still minor, and where her own mental state (which releases adrenaline in tiny amounts) might give her a window to calm the sympathetic/parasympathetic imbalance. That’s a genuine skill. But calling that “controlling an attack” is like saying you can stop a tsunami by building a sandcastle — it works only because the wave isn’t actually here yet. When it is, you need a medicine that directly tells the muscle to release its grip, and no amount of discipline can speak that language.
